[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22289d9c-3dfd-520e-8ec9-85bc4bc255ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22289d9c-3dfd-520e-8ec9-85bc4bc255ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293078} :answer-field/id #uuid "37fe7db5-2328-51fb-b3c8-d7371f09cc13" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "10040f3a-1927-5f73-8f48-332cfb78c0f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-281332" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $25,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5})(x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5}\\right)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{25}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{25} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $25.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $25.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{25}) & =0 \\\\ 25{x}^{2}+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f11d440b-702e-59bc-b92d-6a06a7c710ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{x}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f11d440b-702e-59bc-b92d-6a06a7c710ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{x}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293867} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e5f1550-5e05-5068-9ad0-2f7656e96be8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d6f8e661-f438-5c1a-9465-785304aa2cfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-281333" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $16,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4})(x+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4}\\right)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{6}{16}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{8} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $16.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $16.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{8}) & =0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "206ed248-3cd2-539e-b50d-49612fa287d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "206ed248-3cd2-539e-b50d-49612fa287d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293070} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa4f1cc-54f8-508d-a192-eae9487f5d08" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294378} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3914a06d-7f57-5936-b194-290d53f5ce2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3914a06d-7f57-5936-b194-290d53f5ce2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293170} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1c84dcb-89ee-5e21-8ea6-ed91a4b53259" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294723} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ef8b931-9b98-5d0c-99d9-ea541bd11cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ef8b931-9b98-5d0c-99d9-ea541bd11cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293561} :answer-field/id #uuid "e719e1d2-40c8-5431-8d76-a930299c3197" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ebbf8822-5fa0-5a01-b2fc-91f9cedbb9cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-281977" :question/problem "Let $u=⟨\\frac{2}{3},0,1⟩$ and $v=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$\n\n$u\\times v=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((0)(-3)-(1)(2))i-(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)(-3)-(1)(1))j+(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)(2)-(0)(1))k \\\\  & =(0-2)i-(-2-1)j+\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)k \\\\  & =-2i+3j+\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)k \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,\\frac{4}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295269 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96da5639-b1ab-5764-ba59-7e12ce5f9525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{5}x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293524} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c3ebe1-76b4-534e-ac97-16940002c63a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}x-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293692} {:answer/id #uuid "d426e426-55ae-5a3a-aa02-c573670e3364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293751} {:answer/id #uuid "d741d61c-f686-5e74-9421-30cd24022c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293765} {:answer/id #uuid "fcbe027c-6c76-580a-9173-eaad9074aa07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d426e426-55ae-5a3a-aa02-c573670e3364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293751} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c87e56a-6a3b-50b2-a894-d68a942d6fc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7542d07b-ac7a-53fd-84e7-5ac011364c8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-28338" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $4x+10y=20?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $4x+10y=20,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+10y & =20 \\\\ 10y & =-4x+20 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{10}x+\\frac{20}{10} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{5}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{5}{2}x-4$ has a slope of $\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{5}{2}x-4$ is perpendicular to the given line."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297159 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b268833-a892-5a14-ab75-38558f16bc57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295511} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7be3a8-94b4-5a24-adb6-b753140ab093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296278} {:answer/id #uuid "d19d31e7-9654-53a0-8391-14a1f9103d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296372} {:answer/id #uuid "d427a2bc-12c0-5bdc-b3d1-7a4dbeb358d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296383} {:answer/id #uuid "edf73d42-1443-5359-8707-64a0a6a850c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d427a2bc-12c0-5bdc-b3d1-7a4dbeb358d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296383} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0375b64-7629-58c5-bdc5-3b40af6de210" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "88cefa50-35a4-5398-bbcf-d41d22641c1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-28556" :question/problem "Find ${g}^{″}(4)$ if $g(x)=4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x}-2x+7.$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={(4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x}-2x+7)}^{′} \\\\  & ={(4{x}^{5/2}-2x+7)}^{′} \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right){x}^{3/2}-2+0 \\\\  & =10{x}^{3/2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & ={(10{x}^{3/2}-2)}^{′} \\\\  & =10\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right){x}^{1/2}-0 \\\\  & =15{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & =15\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the second derivative at $x=4$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(4) & =15\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =15(2) \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0480da11-736f-54eb-8f1c-b45c5eafb77b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295288} {:answer/id #uuid "656e07a7-3c98-59bf-85d3-2d52b695e711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295845} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf163c6-73b7-53e4-8558-870d5120e61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296098} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e00c3b-4340-5c91-be7a-047cb1624ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296136} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fe8de4-4953-53e2-babf-0316522289bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0480da11-736f-54eb-8f1c-b45c5eafb77b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295288} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b048a01-7c6a-5074-8040-31f2a7ab3a71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0b4e552-dbf9-536f-981b-a75efbb821de" :question/math-academy-id "q-28655" :question/problem "Find ${h}^{′}(0)$ when $h(x)={5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $h$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nh={5}^{u},\\,u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\,v=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}u}={5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=\\frac{1}{v},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${h}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(0) & =-{5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\  & =-{5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7eb7a0-9e0b-5f24-9b6e-32d423c91838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295328} {:answer/id #uuid "23b932bc-94d0-5090-8141-409a5663c61e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295461} {:answer/id #uuid "4b71a211-d079-5dee-8bfc-012004c98779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295696} {:answer/id #uuid "a88803b8-c592-548a-a2d0-3411a63af750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296152} {:answer/id #uuid "fb317109-fe2c-5752-aced-8c8fe96089ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b71a211-d079-5dee-8bfc-012004c98779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295696} :answer-field/id #uuid "36407ae9-d72f-5658-821c-790738a7cf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b48b10b3-0d9d-5485-bda2-3198c7563d19" :question/math-academy-id "q-28902" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1=3$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1 & =3 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/31/318eba038feb091d6e3adb80ddf325de6039bb331a7713cf1e2c02171a6388d4.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f3c3d8-8906-5dcc-b925-c5044fee995d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295293} {:answer/id #uuid "172bd038-83f1-50f7-9b4f-ec3dc38700f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295405} {:answer/id #uuid "485ff5e9-567a-58ff-8972-5329b142e5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295681} {:answer/id #uuid "5e85b4cd-babf-54c6-9a1b-52db2dcc1e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,175" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295812} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae7842b-8f4f-5df6-ad6b-f519d99383d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e85b4cd-babf-54c6-9a1b-52db2dcc1e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,175" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295812} :answer-field/id #uuid "285833ee-97ac-59f5-929a-9e35139822cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "afa93ab3-2677-50cc-b4ef-12db62bfbb63" :question/math-academy-id "q-28993" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/c7/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={45}^{∘},{315}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=45\\cdot 315=14\\,175.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c52fa2-c36a-54ee-babf-4c74df9b7c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295462} {:answer/id #uuid "94f3efb6-de19-5ba6-a738-f7a0df645543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296061} {:answer/id #uuid "db42d83b-613d-5d87-87b6-086382f3d163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296424} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc7903f-8aa1-5eea-b4b0-5e319e0256dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296431} {:answer/id #uuid "e313e6c1-db2b-5df6-8508-41788ce2b8ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23c52fa2-c36a-54ee-babf-4c74df9b7c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295462} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1c5c21e-8379-522a-9e0a-3a9793c042ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f64999ff-874c-549e-b51f-2404ee85f7ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-29186" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)={120}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${120}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={120}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/ec/ec1135cb0a4d35130a1c11baf9f421a3cdb4bc22cc1b436686dce722fa3c812e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x={120}^{∘},{240}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=120\\cdot 240=28\\,800.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20925ec7-7f41-57da-a6a0-92add3062180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,194" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295449} {:answer/id #uuid "280b78e0-5f5a-574c-a466-ec184573e258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295488} {:answer/id #uuid "5d14f667-ba6c-54e8-ad87-e57b9f7bf6cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,564" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295804} {:answer/id #uuid "6c34c7da-7bd9-5454-940e-63510b5c1702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,164" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295868} {:answer/id #uuid "7b368b4e-74fd-5474-a3c1-4ad34d2797e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,134" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c34c7da-7bd9-5454-940e-63510b5c1702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,164" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295868} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b086743-5f60-58ee-b24a-de350e66ea17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb45da11-83cf-50bf-9d9f-98689c5db9f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-29247" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation  $3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=20-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =20-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =20 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)=(11.309\\,9…)^{∘}≈{11.310}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${11.310}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={11.310}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+{11.310}^{∘}={191.310}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n11.310\\cdot 191.310≈2\\,164\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c8730f-4da0-523c-a116-aa089bfc5975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9b/9b81bfc76324d485561a6be2ea48497bff6364a9ef178ea0f3539ab593f27740.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295418} {:answer/id #uuid "41a5e6d3-729b-5cc9-9a25-19b80fc878c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/564e255c25aa4bd7b54edc51f074f1ddec658f34ea16e148969a748cf9db97be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295641} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad182c2-274a-55fd-8d9b-311ed3d26204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/90a24857a3d932c28d34e86fb5d012ba1e7228c9b8ec34665751f5fff15d4dca.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296093} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb789b6-62e2-5caa-b2d2-6f76d5a38370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/69/6980907e5b0acb4cc8704ed4146da01e8960b41fb32355d4cea54c66abe1d058.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296114} {:answer/id #uuid "d83594e9-dc63-5557-b345-7d779b089d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6d8e707652e4cd73d2cbd55e000a0f41aeee8b5a10ee58f9f2cd2755aed164c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ad182c2-274a-55fd-8d9b-311ed3d26204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/90a24857a3d932c28d34e86fb5d012ba1e7228c9b8ec34665751f5fff15d4dca.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296093} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3fc125f-bb42-568f-b09a-961d791bbb10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a890f17e-1965-548f-8730-d240f1dabda9" :question/math-academy-id "q-29378" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of  $y=-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}-6?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/7b/7b9f7e5ca81a615cb16ccb7a69f9d1669366944d0615041ed8e960f59b8e24dd.png)\n\n- Reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/00/00e5e14399b9fd259327e1c8fbbccd43018df8a4c78b7ed7d957819c81eef7f2.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $6$ units down to give $y=-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}-6.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(-1,-7)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from from $y=0$ to $y=-6.$\n\n![](images/c9/c99dc12a5a34672fa5b5bd180d777c1640b657d0725d115b9f6decfab6296b18.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d06706-2c78-5157-8b7b-53b295f1f0ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/34/349dea8cdd8c309ef83c6f96dcdd27d2f8208758368805bbcd9ca9eac89ee39f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295492} {:answer/id #uuid "6d94d6df-37b8-51c1-8e2b-7d55bac2406e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cc/cc68aab2171488e95ba4904990798de8acc9ca638b813f8adcdabf99112b580c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295873} {:answer/id #uuid "9547e3ca-fcd7-583b-830e-7a376e0c80be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d3/d32cd32e50b81d8a4dbb1c78fefd19d52e5f290d8b31034b6348f88651351f12.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296064} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b347bb-58c5-59b9-80a3-6fee0c54236d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d8/d8201cf21e4a8e5d89695d5a6805a48da01e2c94058f88a8ae179b217f281f89.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296312} {:answer/id #uuid "e87bcc7c-2786-59a2-9f23-610197a13b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c3/c3683c6463c1d7ba1e8d5ef7156a82d8f5c49424b0e6d085230f6d240705869d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9547e3ca-fcd7-583b-830e-7a376e0c80be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d3/d32cd32e50b81d8a4dbb1c78fefd19d52e5f290d8b31034b6348f88651351f12.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296064} :answer-field/id #uuid "d52740c2-cc43-5f72-8ea8-a407686d39b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a8cfa2c-147b-5173-8f78-6a88b030a1e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-29380" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of the function $y=-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x+4}?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/40/401c0b3fea9c98884cf8678bc33ff6442a66f3e766ba89c26917a172b21cfbac.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $4$ units to the left to give $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x+4}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-4,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1487a47526f4c9f546edb8911ed174bdebe80952ee226b0d59f7ac78a122c18.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x+4}.$ The point $(-4,1)$ moves to $(-4,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/52/520a39616c45b8c7fbe389057015d47b7f2270960b426614606596043451ce8d.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144a6036-1a43-5c55-bd4d-8c3b921ab81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295382} {:answer/id #uuid "1a4bc189-56e8-506b-afae-8c32583fd169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295423} {:answer/id #uuid "4809f8aa-d435-582b-a813-64757ceea05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295678} {:answer/id #uuid "598e8e48-0407-5a12-b4c7-8a90c76b22d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295784} {:answer/id #uuid "addb0989-293c-52f5-817b-1eac8428806e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "144a6036-1a43-5c55-bd4d-8c3b921ab81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295382} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0a2dd69-37ef-5e8e-92ea-d1b66a49d028" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "380886ef-0fbf-54bc-9579-e4e21dd4f7ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-29673" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/2+1}}{-\\frac{5}{2}+1}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3/2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{3}}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}-\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{1})^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b9bbfdc-3b39-55c1-8679-95e534fa33dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295429} {:answer/id #uuid "683d53be-c052-56f7-861d-76092b6d9154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295853} {:answer/id #uuid "947b7e02-0117-519f-a1c9-dfce7e8d0f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296060} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2925b9-912c-555c-9907-db502ad42299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296435} {:answer/id #uuid "e041ea49-41c9-58c9-b12c-fc42b38f20b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e041ea49-41c9-58c9-b12c-fc42b38f20b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296457} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0f31d82-50fd-5a69-b8e0-c2f39b910d75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44692078-9fa6-5b9b-a4ac-0bb1d6759de1" :question/math-academy-id "q-29692" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+2=-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+22$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+2 & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+22 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =20 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=0.244\\,978≈0.245\\,0\n$$\n\nSince $0.245\\,0$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.245\\,0.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=-\\pi +x=-\\pi +0.245\\,0=-2.896\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=0.245\\,0,-2.896\\,6.$\n\nFinally, the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n0.245\\,0\\cdot (-2.896\\,6)≈-0.7\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04428be0-686e-5e54-8f4a-3feb9541a499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295286} {:answer/id #uuid "20f44553-3c6b-546a-bd20-ddf483dd3f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295451} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac3b511-b33d-502f-ac79-1ac7b15d0fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295937} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb81092-39a4-56f9-85a7-1c077bd22073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296610} {:answer/id #uuid "ff804d83-8855-560a-a7d7-c75ff6ee34fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff804d83-8855-560a-a7d7-c75ff6ee34fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296634} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb764e43-8a01-5508-9ece-68c4eebbf1dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f0fe807-1fba-5baa-b6b7-6741269e860e" :question/math-academy-id "q-29739" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<1 \\\\ 6-5x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}(6-5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)|}_{0}^{1}+{(6x-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(0)^{3}}{3}]+(6(2)-\\frac{5(2)^{2}}{2}-6(1)+\\frac{5(1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}]+[12-10-6+\\frac{5}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24759908-8693-5af7-bf3d-2bdf229adfe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295466} {:answer/id #uuid "31ff3ce2-0f9d-5616-9e5e-70b00c4eb74c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295549} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf0eb68-b859-542f-97b2-74abf3ccea80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295615} {:answer/id #uuid "54cd9910-13ac-5b07-bea1-7ac02cf40496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295757} {:answer/id #uuid "710f0cf2-c58d-57ff-aeae-670729175369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cf0eb68-b859-542f-97b2-74abf3ccea80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295615} :answer-field/id #uuid "2793591c-e448-524b-b2f1-749711977aa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "732288fe-6fc0-55c4-91c2-b913a4ca0e96" :question/math-academy-id "q-29979" :question/problem "![](images/35/355d3770234a7b0c9a65965d65ad3807adedc7e9c6c8a23ddbe25bb34a0dd5d0.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and the line $y=-2.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2.$ \n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arccos}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)={120}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/ec/ec1135cb0a4d35130a1c11baf9f421a3cdb4bc22cc1b436686dce722fa3c812e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is \n\n$$\nx={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8a397c6-40a7-5813-918a-ce66ebac0ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8a397c6-40a7-5813-918a-ce66ebac0ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296589} :answer-field/id #uuid "85c4f980-7f4a-5ed6-8beb-b91f35640e2b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d93923b-2491-5385-9e01-fb8b3e2e0923" :question/math-academy-id "q-302056" :question/problem "If $p(x)=-5{x}^{2}$ and $q(x)=9{x}^{6},$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{4}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{4}\\cdot (-5{x}^{2})=-5{x}^{6}\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}}{(9{x}^{6})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{5}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54a84d11-9f1b-5569-b5a4-4e973dc2de57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54a84d11-9f1b-5569-b5a4-4e973dc2de57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295755} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c583137-1476-5660-bd11-45c25587d7fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "081bea40-3640-5a40-9984-5a664f4f2206" :question/math-academy-id "q-302650" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}{\\pi x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}{\\pi x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)}{\\pi \\cdot (1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ecd9f88-9441-58a6-b4b8-89da5a9b36d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ecd9f88-9441-58a6-b4b8-89da5a9b36d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295441} :answer-field/id #uuid "363b88c1-f8ed-537e-a7bd-b476c6701812" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "58f94bd2-6052-5c0f-852e-d8d0ce5e7319" :question/math-academy-id "q-302689" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2))=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2):$\n\n![](images/a7/a7e36a5c534a63199ea97985f1301db0c7675adc8fa0d17f33735c61eec9e871.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4675341d-47de-58d1-9169-e4ea300f6534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295666} {:answer/id #uuid "49060b70-8ca0-5560-a25f-c33fdf13abe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295684} {:answer/id #uuid "73e82961-671e-5f43-927c-85b68cebb79e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295902} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc5e39c-b266-5cff-bbd7-b9a7c961e739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296018} {:answer/id #uuid "902705ff-4eac-5fc0-8d8a-b9ebd4cdfeff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4675341d-47de-58d1-9169-e4ea300f6534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295666} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c5828d6-8af8-5472-82db-0c5fb62611d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7ade3ef7-2470-5279-802e-68d9ba79206f" :question/math-academy-id "q-3038" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(1/x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(1/x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe4953c-0983-5464-9362-45c9474d1210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fe4953c-0983-5464-9362-45c9474d1210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295360} :answer-field/id #uuid "91224f8e-576e-5980-8358-902f8e6b2203" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "251ee9eb-ffd1-5d74-9058-3b8ed977eb92" :question/math-academy-id "q-304068" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{3x+2}{{x}^{2}}$ at the point $(-1,-1).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3x+2}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3x+2}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{-1})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{-2}-4{x}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(-1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-\\frac{3}{(-1)^{2}}-\\frac{4}{(-1)^{3}} \\\\  & =-3+4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=1$ and the coordinates of our point $(-1,-1),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =1\\cdot (x-(-1)) \\\\ y+1 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297294 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eac189c-328d-5802-95c5-9c96ec16f319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295354} {:answer/id #uuid "52502f39-c1db-5729-b1b4-23cbf09e92b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,653" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295741} {:answer/id #uuid "76de8925-dcbd-5485-9ac7-782ad20c2c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,894" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295914} {:answer/id #uuid "e6025cbe-ed40-5f72-a8b6-055c9bc77808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,876" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296493} {:answer/id #uuid "f5fc864d-d078-56a5-85da-2d8423488f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23\\,642" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5fc864d-d078-56a5-85da-2d8423488f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23\\,642" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296573} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8da0b69-fc32-5f0e-b379-b274dd917724" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "58af19f4-fcf8-555c-b281-db48de8422e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-30468" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $4(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-4)=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-4) & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-16 & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)=86.416\\,678{…}^{∘}≈{86.417}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${86.417}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={86.417}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={86.417}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={86.417}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/15/15e99d348206191535a2b6fb9d85893e666abd16f5f9fc61fdf4e19424e2fde9.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{86.417}^{∘} \\\\  & ={273.583}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=86.417\\cdot 273.583≈23\\,642,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275b6be7-476f-5664-8d4c-fb7a3832606d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295483} {:answer/id #uuid "84433299-4f1f-5551-8f4d-432c1612da82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295975} {:answer/id #uuid "93487fcb-1176-5aaf-b67c-c9275110a880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296055} {:answer/id #uuid "a49b7592-7b1d-5172-a1d6-a14ce8a1fe9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296134} {:answer/id #uuid "bbae1e1c-4c20-53de-80d8-7f1ca34b8c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a49b7592-7b1d-5172-a1d6-a14ce8a1fe9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296134} :answer-field/id #uuid "021cad1d-dfec-5c2f-a8de-ca1eb6ba39db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90ca11f6-8893-5beb-98a2-24781da24a2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-30588" :question/problem "$\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =3 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a5aa548-8e4a-5a2d-97f6-5c499bd890a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295786} {:answer/id #uuid "7dde1994-c71e-5ef1-be9b-f95aa1f829ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295953} {:answer/id #uuid "86464462-6cf6-5c92-b8e0-b635825871a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295989} {:answer/id #uuid "e3dcad0b-7433-5fda-bfa5-de31b530e4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296482} {:answer/id #uuid "f56f3fda-93e0-5eb4-af15-a675894133b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a5aa548-8e4a-5a2d-97f6-5c499bd890a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295786} :answer-field/id #uuid "29249e25-9c6c-58de-9fd8-1d3099e0c4de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87e893b1-43cc-5695-982d-b173b104605b" :question/math-academy-id "q-30893" :question/problem "Within the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ determine the average value of $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)))] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right))-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right))] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[(0+(-1))-(0+1)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc7422a-6560-5142-961f-48d22cd38ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220+\\frac{5}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295348} {:answer/id #uuid "2c04d99b-a86b-5d68-b6cf-6a1d89b39f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200+\\frac{16}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295523} {:answer/id #uuid "9673e7a1-5472-5c11-b9c9-7778a76c10d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "210-\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296069} {:answer/id #uuid "fadab96a-3e8b-5396-9d82-c76e16597282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200-\\frac{12}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296597} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2a7330-8974-56a8-8299-e0f9004a7e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "230-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c04d99b-a86b-5d68-b6cf-6a1d89b39f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200+\\frac{16}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295523} :answer-field/id #uuid "75acc2e4-2a05-516c-974d-72e69e3612d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c3d43bb3-dee9-539e-921d-9e390abc8b05" :question/math-academy-id "q-30903" :question/problem "A biologist found that the population $P$ of some bacteria in a petri dish can be modeled by the function $P(t)=200+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}t),$ where $t$ is the number of hours after $12$ noon. Find the average number of bacteria during the period from $12$ noon to $4$ p.m." :question/worked-solution "Since the function is valid starting from $12$ P.M., we can conclude our interval is $[0,4].$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}P(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-0}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(200+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}t))\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{4}(200t-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\,t}{4}\\right))|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(200\\cdot 4-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot 4}{4}\\right))-(200\\cdot 0-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot 0}{4}\\right))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(800-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(800-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\cdot (-1))+\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[800+\\frac{32}{\\pi }+\\frac{32}{\\pi }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(800+\\frac{64}{\\pi }) \\\\  & =200+\\frac{16}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a68846e-8392-5a94-a3aa-2686ed8da072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295320} {:answer/id #uuid "2b89a108-9830-5765-ab37-236b6c7a436c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{75}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295517} {:answer/id #uuid "567f2736-63ee-5f2a-b291-57227aca1813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295771} {:answer/id #uuid "98a360bd-7dc7-5172-9965-33a5e01d2144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{75}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296086} {:answer/id #uuid "b68c6e0c-5bad-5bf2-bfb7-247965fb865b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b89a108-9830-5765-ab37-236b6c7a436c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{75}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295517} :answer-field/id #uuid "f97a2636-5f46-5511-ac10-6484a8f5f52e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38f9635e-72c4-5968-9945-fe2ceda9691e" :question/math-academy-id "q-30922" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-5{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=-2x,$ then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-5{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-5{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-5{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{u}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{-2})}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}({e}^{u}){|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{-2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{-2})}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}({4}^{-2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(\\frac{1}{16}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{15}{16}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{75}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297314 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b95ba0-8776-5e87-8a07-fb4dd6e251e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{728}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295276} {:answer/id #uuid "36229afc-ab9a-5470-be3f-a37b681ee81b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{364}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295571} {:answer/id #uuid "43ef6c71-98a2-5c12-8945-04c4d08e3cc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{364}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295649} {:answer/id #uuid "72e3fae5-ccad-59b0-8fc0-ac0e46bfbfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{364}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295895} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc47e71-ee79-5542-acba-c30ea159db79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72e3fae5-ccad-59b0-8fc0-ac0e46bfbfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{364}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295895} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcf773c9-a379-54a1-a3f0-7de93b756309" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34a4e0e2-73c5-54dc-8277-4607c07e4179" :question/math-academy-id "q-30973" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=6x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of  $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{3}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{3}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\left(\\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right){|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(729-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{728}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{364}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e6eb30f-5bfc-5e1d-a01c-0f6eb7e00e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e6eb30f-5bfc-5e1d-a01c-0f6eb7e00e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295719} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8643e81-9ec6-59f8-9881-f0c74f9799e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c450049-c1ac-524d-8d79-0a2c2ad034fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-310180" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is given by ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,u=3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is \n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5668b6e0-5070-5926-b2a2-b7c6f55794fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2c/2c76d1b17d4de159270940297e88ee36a4dc1f95911762bb718107b9500600cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295770} {:answer/id #uuid "67c81233-fa71-5edd-b7c8-dc3764c577d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/65/65850e7d7126c75e4b19e5e30486db97cd8c6a57be2b90b6a2a4748e79e5a279.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295850} {:answer/id #uuid "7b805af7-1cdc-5d84-9081-e540ec84d8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/57f44c1372a4bbb72699a9acdd86b89946cb62885b46fb22e3cdadf52236507a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295943} {:answer/id #uuid "8f802102-3977-5a00-9c3b-326b2bdc389e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d7/d78d3b7474b4a106ac526cbcabbb36472a4a0f9079a4949f031c53e8d92ea581.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296033} {:answer/id #uuid "b6fba35e-9429-559d-a4ac-70eae6265468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/18/18473b0d84a7646e2c3ffe8a71c86f28013c16f85fe633f23a889e34fe43b742.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6fba35e-9429-559d-a4ac-70eae6265468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/18/18473b0d84a7646e2c3ffe8a71c86f28013c16f85fe633f23a889e34fe43b742.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296227} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0b8de79-6c6d-5693-8bc8-24ca752592df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c134dba9-db81-5965-88cc-1e938dfc4f3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-31122" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)}^{x}+4.5?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/7a/7a630b4582049bf2a2144ce484069a5301e7363a35da38e7d86ff2fee22543d4.png)\n\nNext, we shift this graph $4.5$ units up. \nThe horizontal asymptote under such a transformation becomes $y=4.5,$ the $y$-intercept becomes $(0,5.5).$\nThe resulting graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)}^{x}+4.5$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/35/35c5c0ef4850371b35fa43b9f2944e98919199bbacf41453973167522cf04f35.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bc81358-db21-5830-828a-7f9553fed2f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bc81358-db21-5830-828a-7f9553fed2f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295430} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8b5dee0-71c5-549f-b377-87004bd31525" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e00a045-7deb-531f-a9c3-4bec570ef120" :question/math-academy-id "q-311277" :question/problem "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=5$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-4$ | $6$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=5.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)|}_{x=5}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)-f(x){g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=5$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)|}_{x=5} & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(5)g(5)-f(5){g}^{′}(5)}{(g(5))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)(-2)-(3)(6)}{(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-18}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd0cb33b-8767-54e7-a818-0bec60f8663e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd0cb33b-8767-54e7-a818-0bec60f8663e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296341} :answer-field/id #uuid "71661f85-5585-5ca0-ad3a-dc9dadf683ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c578341-fd5b-5ea9-b094-029b8faa3d2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-312258" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,0)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y=6x.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y & =6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x) \\\\ [3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3}]-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6-2x{y}^{3} \\\\ (3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6-2x{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6-2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,0)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(0,\\,0)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{6-2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}\\right)}_{(0,\\,0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{3\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c0fdf7b-82b6-5293-b48c-ebb644ea3002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/025dccc1bd6171977e7f313f63d06a45b761c2ddb6bbe399a1f7f36c76d5b13f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295333} {:answer/id #uuid "10b12a3e-908d-50f2-9d26-f792fd78f970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7d9665194c09083e7cfb1b414e29963c1d2baa2f827fc0f04b4f65ce42f04371.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295363} {:answer/id #uuid "24136d66-bc57-5e32-b192-4d62b04aaee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/43b05acd4910721388359024df28bf746b023e2b94930995c191d7fb25c4f082.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295463} {:answer/id #uuid "87a111eb-6346-5b6f-ac7f-64b8b3acdd81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c9e47a7d542909ded097bc7111b23d954b889f6fd5dc90ba8879ce7dd845254f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295997} {:answer/id #uuid "c7853508-f326-5574-b5ea-4b358b0e7ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1a272ee51fdfcf07289c7f61f4ddc615dce59dfc7e0735a41eba058838858f90.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24136d66-bc57-5e32-b192-4d62b04aaee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/43b05acd4910721388359024df28bf746b023e2b94930995c191d7fb25c4f082.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295463} :answer-field/id #uuid "9df89b8a-15ad-5c98-83dd-aeda440d9f30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1709b5ea-f5ce-5d07-be0b-066860df686f" :question/math-academy-id "q-31234" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{x}+10?$" :question/worked-solution "We begin with the graph of ${\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/16/16a1a985e5c0e15bf9d21cd70eaa63887bfa39dd81ef01720af2d1ea5d2b1516.png)\n\nNext, add $10$ to ${\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{x}$ to give ${\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{x}+10$, a vertical shift by $10$ units up. Point $(0,1)$ on ${\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{x}$ becomes $(0,11)$ on ${\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{x}+10.$ The horizontal asymptote becomes $y=10.$ The new graph is shown below.   \n\n \n\n![](images/af/af910cd3774ce1df9455df13bb1cd94354129b1127c921abb04013540889b41a.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297345 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "918ce529-fbbf-5879-a12c-b8ff4969d44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2y-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "918ce529-fbbf-5879-a12c-b8ff4969d44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2y-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296049} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fd03d82-67c6-5e97-bdf5-e0d9e331e1dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33ddb022-5204-5f5d-a7fe-9687a12ba27b" :question/math-academy-id "q-312549" :question/problem "Given that $x={y}^{2}-y,$ express $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $y.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y) \\\\  & =2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2y-1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1862e13-b849-5e94-8d8e-ccd2b47a0706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1862e13-b849-5e94-8d8e-ccd2b47a0706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296289} :answer-field/id #uuid "f44320fa-c45d-54f3-a6a2-915c4b6ca76f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01978fc9-f56f-57cf-8868-52118ee62fe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-312553" :question/problem "Given a curve $x=3y-{y}^{2},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(0,3)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3y-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =3(1)-2y \\\\  & =3-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3-2y}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(0,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(0,3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b6cfa32-6775-5c83-bbef-9147bd202178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b6cfa32-6775-5c83-bbef-9147bd202178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295794} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeb3a075-f47e-5cb5-80a2-3e23e6313a6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19309cb7-ace3-5af0-908b-5924987e950f" :question/math-academy-id "q-312578" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(0,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bedcd8f-d90b-5061-91ac-f10991c0035c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2x\\operatorname{ln}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bedcd8f-d90b-5061-91ac-f10991c0035c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2x\\operatorname{ln}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295522} :answer-field/id #uuid "441576ee-9428-5996-8d80-7db6b8216c3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a8e96d3e-9fca-5093-8b9b-5bb4483b8048" :question/math-academy-id "q-313371" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{2x}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $x{e}^{2x}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and the denominator $v(x)={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)({e}^{2x})-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})}{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{x}-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-2x{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)}{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}(1-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074a9ec9-6bfa-51c3-b293-a2e6c7634c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295302} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8e6cf9-9f11-5e02-a08b-b0ce3fd9f2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295608} {:answer/id #uuid "64d5cac3-e49f-55a4-a517-b40d5927cff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295841} {:answer/id #uuid "748fff53-6b97-5ddb-a2b7-7573ce8fffb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295904} {:answer/id #uuid "c09b0310-1b57-58cd-bbcf-bbf81a785509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b8e6cf9-9f11-5e02-a08b-b0ce3fd9f2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295608} :answer-field/id #uuid "67494433-5316-509e-bbfc-ae0d8a99e4af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d5db746-ab9b-5b5a-bc25-31e21fc4632d" :question/math-academy-id "q-31338" :question/problem "Find the slope of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\pi -3x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is given by ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u=2\\pi -3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is $3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d62ded4-2ee6-52b4-bc69-a4642638d154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295710} {:answer/id #uuid "81a7a75b-3985-56bc-ad9c-0316c2348f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295965} {:answer/id #uuid "9a357d65-23c5-58be-868e-2ac0c3d388e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296090} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c28805-4880-53a7-a205-3cbba072dd28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296298} {:answer/id #uuid "f605d237-0166-587c-b104-dc53781dd11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d62ded4-2ee6-52b4-bc69-a4642638d154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295710} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aaa5d76-a174-5c5f-afc2-1ec32957d4f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab907b07-4062-5dae-90ac-fc279f3c0e29" :question/math-academy-id "q-31340" :question/problem "Find the slope of $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x)$ at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{csc}⁡u,\\,u=5x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot (5) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=-\\pi /2} & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x){|}_{x=-\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is $0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165e0695-743c-53ea-a0bf-08a7ae6a7e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5}{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295400} {:answer/id #uuid "257afa08-d4dd-5c05-84fd-e23c68f3fa03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{11}{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295473} {:answer/id #uuid "47ef2d54-50f3-5c11-adc5-2e9c8c3f349c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295677} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9bbd8f-12ba-5756-ab8b-55bc35e01784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{11}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295803} {:answer/id #uuid "f24f2c93-19bb-5f2c-bb5d-345956dd2577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{5}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c9bbd8f-12ba-5756-ab8b-55bc35e01784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{11}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295803} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb835fc8-dd55-5bbb-9fd4-1f86940f3061" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0865b352-f43e-56b9-8d78-8c3ca9422e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-31363" :question/problem "For the curve $y=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2,$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $2$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}+2=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{0}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{11}{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db558ad2-5cea-5652-8d34-cc67a45effd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db558ad2-5cea-5652-8d34-cc67a45effd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296426} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb2e960b-ca24-52de-9ab6-07dcbceaaebf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd13c00d-5dc7-5ccb-a6d3-56bdaaf16d42" :question/math-academy-id "q-313637" :question/problem "Given that $r=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-3$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-3$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3)\\cdot 5\\cdot (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f61aea39-f97c-5016-8587-a2f49bb65ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{3}{8}{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f61aea39-f97c-5016-8587-a2f49bb65ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{3}{8}{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296575} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fc1501a-a5bd-5b32-9331-155d085aefef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1bab49d0-a1b6-58fc-8a02-9d9fd89dd0f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-313723" :question/problem "Find ${g}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ if $g(x)=csc⁡x-2{x}^{3}+cos⁡x.$\n\n${g}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-csc⁡x-2{x}^{3}+cos⁡x) \\\\  & =(-csc⁡xcot⁡x)-2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+(-sin⁡x) \\\\  & =-csc⁡xcot⁡x-6{x}^{2}-sin⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =-csc\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)cot\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-6{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{2}-sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1-6\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{3}{8}{\\pi }^{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{3}{8}{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f276cdd-ecf5-5a99-899d-51cd15626a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f276cdd-ecf5-5a99-899d-51cd15626a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295357} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5576b47-0128-5872-8fac-e50d447fb770" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f973c2da-4afc-57bc-bbf9-262e7639f470" :question/math-academy-id "q-313938" :question/problem "Consider the section of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1$ that lies in the second quadrant. Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=12$ on the hyperbola at the point where $x=-5,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}\\right)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}\\right)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2x}{9}\\right)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2y}{9}\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=12$ and $x=-5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2(-5)}{9}\\right)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2y}{9}\\right) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{120}{9}-\\frac{2y}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=-5$ into the equation of the hyperbola.\n\nAt the point where $x=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{25}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 25-{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y>0$ since we're only interested in the second quadrant.\n\nFinally, substituting $y=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{120}{9}-\\frac{2(4)}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{120}{9}-\\frac{8}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{120}{9} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{120}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18224ab4-1997-54b1-9d84-46c76f50925a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/749d466c80d05d054b8d661b6c3405c92f904df65ca7c3442746d19f73394834.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295409} {:answer/id #uuid "8b94732d-431a-566b-85ac-520a42fe1feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/62/6212d04b1004e0110d7aff817f03bab0b98f74dd5e334fe6607475ed13fe3fb0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296010} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6e4b30-9549-530f-b9ff-5fc3fbd7b8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6a/6adc189f0d16cf60044553c13d79297c04f4fbfb2806a9bde6971b203822f17a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296266} {:answer/id #uuid "d546bbf6-c8b7-53f3-959d-73a13e4ee107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/53/53d2977f07e132aa0e3c3608e8fef82f2d058fb1a99aa828ae8b317b8de6844a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296389} {:answer/id #uuid "df1d268b-fd3c-5617-8309-bc2ad0fab779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/44/4441bcb0d11698cff76c8e74c7c34a8109523a581eb5810c388e9f17c09e8176.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd6e4b30-9549-530f-b9ff-5fc3fbd7b8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6a/6adc189f0d16cf60044553c13d79297c04f4fbfb2806a9bde6971b203822f17a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296266} :answer-field/id #uuid "7108cc44-6ba9-5062-9231-4d0b0008bcbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbe47114-407d-5b49-92a6-a4e3739775b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-31416" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+1?$" :question/worked-solution "We start with plotting the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/ca/ca12bd6db97d982fd2a9e73dcd9d5da0248ce1a40e582787c3f73480f338a570.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{3}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/53/53d2977f07e132aa0e3c3608e8fef82f2d058fb1a99aa828ae8b317b8de6844a.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+1.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](images/6a/6adc189f0d16cf60044553c13d79297c04f4fbfb2806a9bde6971b203822f17a.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a1c47f7-5b8c-5177-997f-9c9709da23dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295599} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3cb064-2eaf-5f92-9194-de89c9125599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295717} {:answer/id #uuid "7b75ed7d-abd8-526c-8c58-6405c2b625a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295942} {:answer/id #uuid "d96c6138-85f4-575a-bbd3-c78b6c063670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296413} {:answer/id #uuid "f51945cd-cb43-5802-a043-9d995443a3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a1c47f7-5b8c-5177-997f-9c9709da23dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295599} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d0e6b0-fcd1-5675-b95f-02f242a5a3ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b52cd54-7f7c-57a1-892c-73badca0d5f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-31435" :question/problem "![](images/01/016286e82482af83783f0eef6361591861b564643d4d9f010f2b82999fcaad27.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$ Hence, $c=-4.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,-2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}-4 & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{0}-4 & =-2 \\\\ k-4 & =-2 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=2-(-4)=6.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}-4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7e30d62-ad76-58fe-b63e-becec8699503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+5)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7e30d62-ad76-58fe-b63e-becec8699503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+5)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296404} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd994f66-bf62-5f46-b267-9ec2a9a54874" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78ab970d-f949-563c-b0af-4172efd56054" :question/math-academy-id "q-314738" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+5}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+5)(1+4{x}^{6})}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, using the formula for the derivative of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ and applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(({x}^{2}+5)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3}))-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+5))}{({x}^{2}+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(({x}^{2}+5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+(2{x}^{3})^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})\\cdot 2x)}{({x}^{2}+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{({x}^{2}+5)(6{x}^{2})}{1+4{x}^{6}}-2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3}))}{({x}^{2}+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+5)(1+4{x}^{6})}-\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+5)^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76aca1b6-5661-51da-99d2-f8c22f5b96ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76aca1b6-5661-51da-99d2-f8c22f5b96ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295912} :answer-field/id #uuid "317eccc7-ddd1-5b16-9d57-f970d8ad8624" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b48b719-9f12-5c32-8d2e-c9f7b2979bfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-314762" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}{e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\left(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}\\right){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{6}}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}\\,(2-3{x}^{2})\\,{e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}(2-3{x}^{2}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we ignored the negative square root since $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{2}{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{6}}\\,(2-3\\,{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}){e}^{-1/(1/2)^{2}} \\\\  & =128\\,(2-\\frac{3}{4}){e}^{-4} \\\\  & =160{e}^{-4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{2}{1}\\,(2-3)\\,{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}},$ we conclude that $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}$ is indeed a point of inflection."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d116e5-bda3-5bb6-9073-4a756892bfc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{120}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28d116e5-bda3-5bb6-9073-4a756892bfc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{120}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295493} :answer-field/id #uuid "6116ff27-77f4-5dbc-b0a0-eb44a9d20d1f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "69493ee6-a225-5106-b233-5e8265954b40" :question/math-academy-id "q-314900" :question/problem "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=1.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to estimate the value of $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=1,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right) & ≈L\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{30} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{120}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "169ba8d5-e22c-50d2-9733-e76f465346c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295401} {:answer/id #uuid "358520ce-be66-5f40-8a5d-895602d02530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295568} {:answer/id #uuid "38549704-6a61-553b-a029-2c376582ae9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295584} {:answer/id #uuid "6986393b-a4fa-50a9-8106-363feaca3624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295859} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9c92c9-e91d-5425-a408-be18f6225423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "169ba8d5-e22c-50d2-9733-e76f465346c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295401} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f3a5154-fd03-5ed7-b3ff-94e6906bb61f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c446587-25a8-5d97-80ba-a22c604d7eae" :question/math-academy-id "q-31517" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}=$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1]^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d5b2fa6-381a-5d5d-bef8-7d79886dce3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6j}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d5b2fa6-381a-5d5d-bef8-7d79886dce3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6j}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295872} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f7de079-4ed6-5563-b506-283566b45488" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ed8b4ad4-c2df-5a80-8184-0f46540123d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-315701" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-4)$ between $x=2$ and $x=5$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n${S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{3}{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡($\n\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of subintervals, ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$, and  $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nThe endpoints indicate that $a=2$ and $b=5,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(2+j\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{3}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(2+\\frac{3j}{n})-4) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{6j}{n}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b49b220e-9fd5-51d8-96f0-d25d920276ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{j}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b49b220e-9fd5-51d8-96f0-d25d920276ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{j}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296215} :answer-field/id #uuid "67782cc0-027b-537d-a830-7413951d2e11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1487daa-866b-50a7-afd5-9a9671f89a9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-315717" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=(x+2)^{3/2}$ between $x=-2$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $10$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j.$*\n\n${S}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}$\n ${\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}($\n{{field-1}}$)^{3/2}$" :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're given that there are  $n=10$ subintervals.  The endpoints indicate that $a=-2$ and $b=3,$  so the step size is \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-(-2)}{10}=\\frac{5}{10}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{10-1}f(-2+j\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}f\\left(\\frac{2j-4}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}{((-2+\\frac{j}{2})+2)}^{3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}{\\left(\\frac{j}{2}\\right)}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd773e6e-ca28-595a-a29f-9ed27ba023fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd773e6e-ca28-595a-a29f-9ed27ba023fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296444} :answer-field/id #uuid "1da08f0c-44ab-530f-833c-854134378c93" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34fe7280-76bb-5067-ad59-9c70ad7f535a" :question/math-academy-id "q-315824" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b8ca01e64d2830bd5fce6a5c0b47b74f955ae1e4a7e37dcd0c7bcaa41ed1383.png)\n\nA small, circular ink blot spreads on a paper. If the blot’s circumference is increasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$, calculate the rate of change of its area at the moment when the radius is $10\\,\\text{mm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe area of the ink blot is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=10,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=4\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {\\left(\\frac{C}{2\\pi }\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=10\\,\\text{mm},$ $C=2\\pi (10)=20\\pi \\text{ mm}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=4\\pi $ and $C=20\\pi $, and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{20\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =40\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area is increasing at a rate of $40\\pi {\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d295d5b-60d1-5782-8664-c37de7a04be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295952} {:answer/id #uuid "86d84b87-e847-5627-b2c8-7431aca89887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295991} {:answer/id #uuid "9b0c482e-168d-5a8f-b036-192f6be5273d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296094} {:answer/id #uuid "d0586360-51f6-5b81-b5f1-689bad305a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296362} {:answer/id #uuid "ff29c031-3d6e-55e3-8917-ccff8fee18db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86d84b87-e847-5627-b2c8-7431aca89887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295991} :answer-field/id #uuid "97f70dc6-bebf-5d3e-8c79-3942528669e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4cee0a4-4d54-5d5b-88b7-af1da57b4e05" :question/math-academy-id "q-31602" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{-2(1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e041eb96-3c3d-598f-b4fa-1713d77c0792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e041eb96-3c3d-598f-b4fa-1713d77c0792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296458} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fbcd32a-2ead-5e5a-8f5f-ee36b7fb8996" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1881e344-7776-5325-86c9-42068cf4edbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-316023" :question/problem "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(b,2b)$ and $(3,0),$ where $b$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & ={(b-3)}^{2}+{(2b-0)}^{2} \\\\  & =(b-3)^{2}+(2b)^{2} \\\\  & =({b}^{2}-6b+9)+(4{b}^{2}) \\\\  & =5{b}^{2}-6b+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(b)=10b-6.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(b)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =0 \\\\ 10b-6 & =0 \\\\ b & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)=10>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=\\frac{3}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(b)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $b=\\frac{3}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}-6\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{5}-\\frac{18}{5}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(b,2b)$ and $(3,0)$ is $\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53539640-bf8b-506e-bdc0-a98614bbaf75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53539640-bf8b-506e-bdc0-a98614bbaf75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295746} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2f62b30-0d7e-5226-bbfa-f9da2b0d309c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e8278e6-1f98-55c4-88ff-9d0d17e17bd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-316024" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d282e4afd0a66dd79fecf474da9f618a5970197943044d24208f86c4c43781e6.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x{|}_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =-(arcsin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-arcsin\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e03faf3-3e92-514d-a666-b56caff650f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295437} {:answer/id #uuid "ceae20b4-51bc-53f3-a56b-409b71e224ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceae20b4-51bc-53f3-a56b-409b71e224ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516550} :answer-field/id #uuid "46fed9e6-b6f9-583b-951d-71398609b0ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9a63b25-eb4e-543f-9169-7fae6ed27cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-316302" :question/problem "Find the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $f(x)={cos}^{8}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ on the interval $[0,\\pi ].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi -0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{8}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate this integral using a substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{8}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\left(\\frac{{u}^{9}}{9}\\right)|}_{1}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9\\pi }[(-1)^{9}-{1}^{9}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9\\pi }(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "563dad36-6b94-5115-a57a-7ed69e7beda4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "563dad36-6b94-5115-a57a-7ed69e7beda4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295767} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcb2fd8e-6775-59e6-84bb-a5fbb95d6899" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7177af1c-e318-563b-be13-170617488953" :question/math-academy-id "q-316441" :question/problem "Suppose that $|\\,a\\,|=4$, $|\\,b\\,|=8\\sqrt{2}$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Find the vector projection of $b$ onto $a$ in terms of the vector $a.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,b=$\n{{field-1}}\n$a$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =|\\,b\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $a:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a}{|\\,a\\,|}=\\frac{a}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}a\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,b & ={\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b\\cdot u \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}a \\\\  & =2\\,a\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbe8cc4d-79b0-54f4-a4b4-402238d2f931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbe8cc4d-79b0-54f4-a4b4-402238d2f931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296254} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bf316c9-9c3a-5ad3-a098-ccbd0d67af6d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1d1ee30-1190-5b35-acf5-2df3a55be76b" :question/math-academy-id "q-316742" :question/problem "![](images/a9/a988c243bcf4b82cd05b1ed0292e0595141dbe37a470abc34b183f0ce184fe3f.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x+8}-2$, the $y$-axis and the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{x+8}-2 \\\\ y+2 & =\\sqrt[3]{x+8} \\\\ (y+2)^{3} & =x+8 \\\\ x & =(y+2)^{3}-8 \\\\ x & ={y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+12y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+12y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{-2}^{0}\\,+\\,6\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{-2}^{0}+12\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{16}{4})+6(0-\\left(-\\frac{8}{3}\\right))+12(0-\\frac{4}{2}) \\\\  & =-4+16-24 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-12|=12\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5d38772-4b5b-53be-83ea-a1f62864f16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{62}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5d38772-4b5b-53be-83ea-a1f62864f16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{62}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296222} :answer-field/id #uuid "330ce775-48a6-5c82-93ff-bcb27cec017e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296728} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a16a753-9180-57fe-adaf-f6ab1faa0c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a16a753-9180-57fe-adaf-f6ab1faa0c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296007} :answer-field/id #uuid "7522bb3c-ed09-5bbf-8946-9527499dae16" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01844fd5-ba60-50ee-9116-494fb1e9fc7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-316755" :question/problem "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{-6+5\\text{i}}{4+7\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator is $4+7\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $4-7\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $4-7\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-6+5\\text{i}}{4+7\\text{i}} & =\\frac{-6+5\\text{i}}{4+7\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{4-7\\text{i}}{4-7\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6+5\\text{i})(4-7\\text{i})}{(4+7\\text{i})(4-7\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24+42\\text{i}+20\\text{i}-35{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{4}^{2}-(7\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24+62\\text{i}-35(-1)}{{4}^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24+62\\text{i}+35}{16+49} \\\\  & =\\frac{11+62\\text{i}}{65}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11+62\\text{i}}{65}=\\left(\\frac{11}{65}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{62}{65}\\right)\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8af541e-7e35-57c9-93d7-2e97947812a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8af541e-7e35-57c9-93d7-2e97947812a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296590} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce0fe8fd-1f5b-552b-ba53-90cb8031ef83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297060} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bb407ab-2ef1-5e6a-9fc4-9cf1bc5892b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bb407ab-2ef1-5e6a-9fc4-9cf1bc5892b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295865} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaba4ba3-143a-57b1-b5ee-6221d8645cd1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cadbc9e-d855-58f0-9e49-d42247bff4db" :question/math-academy-id "q-316756" :question/problem "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{12-19\\text{i}}{8-11\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator is $8-11\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $8+11\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $8+11\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12-19\\text{i}}{8-11\\text{i}} & =\\frac{12-19\\text{i}}{8-11\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{8+11\\text{i}}{8+11\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(12-19\\text{i})(8+11\\text{i})}{(8-11\\text{i})(8+11\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{96+132\\text{i}-152\\text{i}-209{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{8}^{2}-(11\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{96-20\\text{i}-209(-1)}{{8}^{2}+{11}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{96-20\\text{i}+209}{64+121} \\\\  & =\\frac{305-20\\text{i}}{185} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(61-4\\text{i})}{5\\cdot 37} \\\\  & =\\frac{61-4\\text{i}}{37}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{61-4\\text{i}}{37}=\\left(\\frac{61}{37}\\right)+\\left(-\\frac{4}{37}\\right)\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3e9f52a-05c8-54c3-aea9-0d7a5d09276e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3e9f52a-05c8-54c3-aea9-0d7a5d09276e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296302} :answer-field/id #uuid "23311f9a-c630-55e2-80ec-6d5a5239b6a5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296694} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ccf4a03-07fc-581b-a92f-6d1352c75136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ccf4a03-07fc-581b-a92f-6d1352c75136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295340} :answer-field/id #uuid "92dd9c18-f814-5ba4-aae9-bbd95db3b2de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b6ea44a-cb33-5d3e-be17-0e5b6ad67140" :question/math-academy-id "q-316773" :question/problem "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{6+\\text{i}}{1+3\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$\\,)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator is $1+3\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $1-3\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $1-3\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6+\\text{i}}{1+3\\text{i}} & =\\frac{6+\\text{i}}{1+3\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1-3\\text{i}}{1-3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6+\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i})}{(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-18\\text{i}+\\text{i}-3{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-17\\text{i}-3(-1)}{1-(-9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-17\\text{i}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{9-17\\text{i}}{10}=\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)+\\left(-\\frac{17}{10}\\right)\\,\\text{i}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfcfd111-e3dc-5a1c-a772-ec253bbe1aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfcfd111-e3dc-5a1c-a772-ec253bbe1aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298514} :answer-field/id #uuid "14c2722b-980d-5c5c-adb6-34310fa305dc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298715} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "837ddb7b-7f17-55d7-bb16-e89cd98ecd83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "837ddb7b-7f17-55d7-bb16-e89cd98ecd83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298122} :answer-field/id #uuid "81f55938-b2dc-55e9-971d-937c82b33523" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb2a4759-56e0-536a-934a-f3114c29c895" :question/math-academy-id "q-316847" :question/problem "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{5\\text{i}}{1+3\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator is $1+3\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $1-3\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $1-3\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\text{i}}{1+3\\text{i}} & =\\frac{5\\text{i}}{1+3\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1-3\\text{i}}{1-3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\text{i}-15{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\text{i}-15(-1)}{1-(-9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\text{i}+15}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{15+5\\text{i}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{15+5\\text{i}}{10}=\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{i}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5403295d-b558-506b-af20-07ab9e169a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5403295d-b558-506b-af20-07ab9e169a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297908} :answer-field/id #uuid "1282924a-5cc6-5ab7-b1c0-8b35e67eb4c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298704} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e7e4118-d951-55db-a8f9-9a8b48a7b270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e7e4118-d951-55db-a8f9-9a8b48a7b270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297685} :answer-field/id #uuid "13cf5da1-328e-5d95-b33d-75c2682f138d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a289044a-c00d-5726-8305-f04b6399fd17" :question/math-academy-id "q-316848" :question/problem "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{7\\text{i}}{3-\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator is $3-\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $3+\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $3+\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7\\text{i}}{3-\\text{i}} & =\\frac{7\\text{i}}{3-\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{3+\\text{i}}{3+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{21\\text{i}+7{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{3}^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{21\\text{i}+7(-1)}{9-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7+21\\text{i}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{-7+21\\text{i}}{10}=\\left(-\\frac{7}{10}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{21}{10}\\right)\\,\\text{i}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "894f88b5-7f22-51f3-a168-4f5139f0628c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "894f88b5-7f22-51f3-a168-4f5139f0628c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298148} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f465e0e-a3d4-5ce8-a70e-c5b1543e9e91" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299013} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277cf5cc-f5ef-5538-8c77-89c05d1e4f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "277cf5cc-f5ef-5538-8c77-89c05d1e4f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297717} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4d6c5b3-a9a0-5483-b68d-dcd77ec26711" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d2e7049-1420-58a2-b0dd-c8da4bdbd90b" :question/math-academy-id "q-316850" :question/problem "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{8\\text{i}}{3-4\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator is $3-4\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $3+4\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $3+4\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\text{i}}{3-4\\text{i}} & =\\frac{8\\text{i}}{3-4\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{3+4\\text{i}}{3+4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\text{i}+32{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{3}^{2}-(4\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\text{i}+32(-1)}{9-(-16)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-32+24\\text{i}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{-32+24\\text{i}}{25}=\\left(-\\frac{32}{25}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{24}{25}\\right)\\,\\text{i}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53b4e376-4def-5579-97b4-ea9cfafb9900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53b4e376-4def-5579-97b4-ea9cfafb9900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297905} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b27f05e-7470-5f12-b910-15d67f334f80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298787} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85ff42ef-64da-51eb-8231-6f4fb9b4c552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298130}] :answer-field